# The Post-Mauryan Kingdoms: Indo-Greeks, Kushanas, and Satavahanas

> After the Mauryas fell in 185 BCE no single power held the
> subcontinent. Greek, Shaka, and Central Asian dynasties ruled the
> north-west while the Satavahanas bridged the Deccan — an age that
> fused Greek coinage, Kushana gold, and Roman trade into India.

## 1. A Fragmented Canvas

The two centuries after Pushyamitra Shunga's coup saw a dozen powers
share India: the Shungas and Kanvas near Vidisha and Pataliputra,
Kharavela's Chedis in Kalinga, Indo-Greeks and Shakas in the
north-west, the Kushanas on the northern plains, and the Satavahanas
across the Deccan. Political division, paradoxically, went with
economic integration — coins, guilds, and sea lanes tied India to
China and Rome as never before.

| Power | Core area | Approximate span |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shungas and Kanvas | Vidisha, Pataliputra | c. 185-73 BCE |
| Indo-Greeks | North-west, Sakala | c. 190-50 BCE |
| Shakas (Western Kshatrapas) | Ujjain, Gujarat | c. 70 BCE-400 CE |
| Kushanas | Peshawar, Mathura | c. 1st-3rd century CE |
| Satavahanas | Deccan, Paithan | c. 1st century BCE-225 CE |

[info title="How we know this age"]
The *Periplus of the Erythraean Sea* (about 1st century CE), a Greek
sailor's trading handbook, lists ports and prices; the Puranas give
king-lists; inscriptions like Rudradaman's at Junagadh speak in their
rulers' own words; and coins — struck in their thousands — date
dynasties the texts never name.
[/info]

## 2. The Indo-Greeks: Greeks Who Became Indians

The Bactrian Greek **Demetrius** pushed into the Kabul valley and the
Punjab around 190 BCE, and for a century and a half some thirty
Indo-Greek kings ruled from cities such as Sakala (Sialkot). The
greatest was **Menander** (Milinda), who reigned from about 165 to
145 BCE. In the Pali classic *Milindapanho* ("Questions of King
Milinda") he debates Buddhist doctrine with the monk **Nagasena**
and, by tradition, ends a royal convert.

- Indo-Greek coins were the first in India that can be firmly
  attributed to named kings, with royal portraits, die-striking, and
  bilingual legends in Greek and Prakrit (Kharoshthi or Brahmi).
- Kings adopted Indian ways: Menander embraced Buddhism; Agathocles
  and others put Vishnu's symbols on their money.
- Their presence seeded **Hellenistic art** in Gandhara and Greek
  astronomers and horoscopes into Indian science.

[definition]
**Yavana** — the Sanskrit and Prakrit word (from "Ionian") for Greeks
and, later, any western foreigner. It names the Yavana era (c. 180
BCE), Yavana settlements, and "yavanapriya" — pepper, "dear to the
Greeks", which drew Mediterranean ships to Malabar.
[/definition]

## 3. The Shakas and Parthians

Nomadic Shakas (Scythians), pushed out of Central Asia, entered
India in five branches, of which the **Western Kshatrapas** of
Ujjain and Gujarat proved the most durable — ruling for over three
centuries until Chandragupta II extinguished them around 400 CE.
**Nahapana** (c. 119-124 CE) earned the Satavahana king
Gautamiputra Satakarni's enmity: the victor restruck his rival's
coins, and the Jogalthembi hoard preserves the evidence.

Rudradaman I (c. 130-150 CE) claims in his **Junagadh rock
inscription** — the first long royal text in chaste Sanskrit — that
he twice defeated the Satavahanas yet rebuilt the **Sudarshana
lake**, first excavated for Chandragupta Maurya and improved under
Ashoka. The **Parthians (Pahlavas)** who followed, under
**Gondophernes** (c. 19-45 CE), carry a special legend: Christian
tradition makes St Thomas land in his kingdom, planting the Syrian
churches of Kerala.

## 4. The Kushanas: Chieftains to Emperors

The **Yuechi**, a nomad people driven from the Chinese frontier,
settled the Oxus basin; one of their five clans, the **Kushanas**,
marched south. **Kujula Kadphises** united the five clans; his heir
**Vima Kadphises** issued India's first substantial **gold coins**,
stamped with Shiva and the bull, and traded deep into the Roman
world.

@diagram tree id="kushana-line" layout="tree" aria="The Kushana ruling line from Kujula Kadphises to Vasudeva"
  Kushanas -> "Kujula Kadphises (c. 15-65 CE)"
  Kushanas -> "Vima Kadphises (c. 65-75 CE)"
  Kushanas -> "Kanishka I (reign tied to 78 CE era)"
  Kushanas -> "Later line: Vasishka, Huvishka"
  Kushanas -> "Vasudeva (last great king)"
@/diagram

**Kanishka I**, the most famous Kushana, is remembered for the era
of **78 CE**, which India still uses as the Shaka era. His twin
capitals were **Purushapura (Peshawar)** and **Mathura**; from them
the dynasty straddled the **Silk Route**, taxing the silk that moved
from Han China to Roman Syria. At Kanishka's court sat the poet
**Ashvaghosha** (*Buddhacharita*), the physician **Charaka**
(*Charakasamhita*), the philosopher **Nagarjuna** of the Madhyamika
school, and the monk **Vasumitra** — who presided, with
Ashvaghosha's help, over the **Fourth Buddhist Council** in Kashmir,
where the Sarvastivada canon was written down.

[important title="Gold, silk, and the Roman drain"]
Kushana gold coins copied Roman weight-standards because India's
exports — silk transshipped from China, pepper, pearls, muslin — were
paid for in Roman aurei. Thousands of Roman coins found in India,
and Pliny's complaint that the empire bled some 50 million sesterces
a year to India alone, measure this one-way traffic.
[/important]

## 5. The Satavahanas: A Bridge Across the Deccan

The **Satavahanas** (called Andhras in the Puranas), founded by
**Simuka** about the 1st century BCE, ruled from **Pratishthana**
(modern Paithan) over the Deccan plateau, linking the Mauryan north
with the Tamil south. Their greatest king, **Gautamiputra Satakarni**
(c. 106-130 CE), destroyed Nahapana's Shaka line; his mother
**Gautami Balashri's** Nashik inscription crowns him with the title
**Trisamudratoyapitavahana** — "whose horses drink at the waters of
the three seas".

@diagram tree id="satavahana-line" layout="tree" aria="Key Satavahana rulers from Simuka to Yajna Sri Satakarni"
  Satavahanas -> "Simuka (c. 1st century BCE)"
  Satavahanas -> "Hala (Gathasaptasati poet-king)"
  Satavahanas -> "Gautamiputra Satakarni (c. 106-130 CE)"
  Satavahanas -> "Vashishthiputra Pulumayi"
  Satavahanas -> "Yajna Sri Satakarni (last great king)"
@/diagram

- The Satavahanas issued **lead and potin coins** — Deccan mines
  supplied the metal — and Yajna Sri's coins carry a **ship**,
  the clearest sign of sea-trade pride.
- They made India's first recorded **land grants to Brahmanas**
  (Nashik and Karle inscriptions), a practice that would reshape
  medieval landholding.
- Kings bore **matronymics** (Gautamiputra, Vashishthiputra) —
  mothers' names — hinting at high female status in the Deccan.
- Their Prakrit court produced **Hala's Gathasaptasati**, 700 love
  lyrics, and the tradition of **Gunadhya's Brihatkatha**.
- Early **Ajanta, Karle, Bhaja, and Nashik caves** — chaityas and
  viharas cut for Buddhist monks — are Satavahana patronage.

## 6. Administration and Society

Satavahana officers included the **amatya** and **mahamatra**;
provinces (*aharas*) went to princes, districts to officials who
checked the guilds. The Kushanas ran a looser, more military state:
the "Great Kushana" styled himself *daivaputra* (son of heaven, a
Chinese title), *shahi*, and *devaputra* (son of the gods) at once,
ruling through satraps and strategic marriage alliances.

[definition]
**Nigama and shreni** — the Deccan's trade guilds. Guilds minted
some Satavahana coins, ran their own seals, set quality standards,
and lent money to temples and monks; the state taxed their turnover
rather than micromanaging trade.
[/definition]

@chart bar title="Approximate duration of rule (years)" labels:
Indo-Greeks
Western Kshatrapas
Kushanas
Satavahanas

