@/chart

## 7. Trade with Rome and the Spice Route

The *Periplus* names the ports: **Bharukachchha** (Broach/Barygaza)
and **Sopara** on the west coast, **Muziris** on the Malabar pepper
coast, **Kaveripattinam** in the Chola land, and **Arikamedu**
(Poduke) near Pondicherry. Excavations at Arikamedu by Mortimer
Wheeler in 1945 found **Arretine ware**, wine amphorae, and Roman
lamps amid Tamil bead-making — a Roman trading station in all but
name.

| Exports from India | Imports from Rome |
| --- | --- |
| Black pepper, ginger, cardamom | Wine, olive oil |
| Pearls, ivory, muslin | Coral, glassware, topaz |
| Silk, precious stones, tortoiseshell | Gold and silver coins |

A Pandya embassy reached the court of **Augustus** (Strabo records
envoys about 20 BCE); a Tamil king sent a gift of slaves and a
tiger to the emperor. The traffic ran the other way in coins:
Roman aurei of Augustus and Tiberius occur in hoards across the
south, paying for the pepper fleet that Pliny said drained Rome's
treasury.

## 8. Art and Religion: Three Schools

| School | Region | Material and style |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Gandhara | Peshawar valley, Taxila | Blue-grey schist; Greco-Roman drapery, wavy hair |
| Mathura | Mathura, Sarnath | Red spotted sandstone; indigenous, vigorous |
| Amaravati | Krishna valley | White marble; narrative friezes |

Kanishka patronised Buddhism in its new **Mahayana** form, whose
bodhisattva ideal suited devotional ages; the first iconic images of
the Buddha appear at Mathura and in Gandhara roughly together, and
Kanishka's Peshawar stupa — its engineering overseen by the Greek
**Agisilaos** — was accounted among the marvels of Asia by Chinese
pilgrims. Hindu gods shared the same coins: Vima Kadphises stamped
Shiva on gold, and the Mathura school carved both Buddhas and
Vishnu.

[note]
The period's genius was translation rather than conquest: Greek
dies served Indian gods, Central Asian kings adopted Sanskrit and
Prakrit titles, and Satavahana grants Latin and Greek trading
partners. Rome, China, and India met on the Kushana stretch of the
Silk Route.
[/note]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| Menander's dialogue | Milindapanho, with monk Nagasena |
| First gold coins | Vima Kadphises (Kushana) |
| Kanishka's era | 78 CE, later the Shaka era |
| Fourth Buddhist Council | Kashmir, Sarvastivada canon |
| Gautamiputra's title | Trisamudratoyapitavahana |
| First Sanskrit royal inscription | Rudradaman, Junagadh |
| Sudarshana lake | Rebuilt by Rudradaman |
| Gathasaptasati | Hala, in Prakrit |
| Roman trade ports | Broach, Muziris, Arikamedu |
| Three art schools | Gandhara, Mathura, Amaravati |
